1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,452
How do we know whether a card is Uzcard or Humo, and where do we keep its number?

2
00:00:04,452 --> 00:00:07,857
The first digits of a card, its BIN, tell us the scheme.

3
00:00:07,857 --> 00:00:12,273
A scheduled job keeps the BIN registry in sync with both schemes.

4
00:00:12,273 --> 00:00:16,357
A plain card number in our database would be a disaster on the day of a breach.

5
00:00:16,357 --> 00:00:22,547
So the card vault encrypts the number with AES-GCM, and gives everyone else a token instead.

6
00:00:22,547 --> 00:00:28,011
Only the vault can turn a token back into a card number, and only to send it to the processing center.

7
00:00:28,011 --> 00:00:30,785
Every reveal is written to an audit trail.

8
00:00:30,785 --> 00:00:35,511
A stolen database now holds nothing but ciphertext and meaningless tokens.

9
00:00:35,511 --> 00:00:40,416
Next, the payment itself, starting with a request nobody can forge.

